{"records":[{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9w4g54bv43","title":"Genome-wide screens reveal that resveratrol induces replicative stress in human cells","description":"Resveratrol is a natural product associated with wide-ranging effects in animal and cellular models including lifespan extension. To identify the genetic target of resveratrol in human cells, we conducted genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screens to pinpoint genes that confer sensitivity or resistance to resveratrol. An extensive network of DNA damage response and replicative stress genes exhibited genetic interactions with resveratrol and its analog pterostilbene. These genetic profiles showed similarity to the response to hydroxyurea, an inhibitor of ribonucleotide reductase that causes replicative stress. Resveratrol, pterostilbene and hydroxyurea caused similar depletion of nucleotide pools, inhibition of replication fork progression and induction of replicative stress. The ability of resveratrol to inhibit cell proliferation and S phase transit was independent of the histone deacetylase Sirtuin 1, which has been implicated in lifespan extension by resveratrol. These results establish that a primary impact of resveratrol on human cell proliferation is the induction of low-level replicative stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-04T14:56:19.488Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9w4g54bv43","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9w4g54bv43"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yahya","last_name":"Benslimane","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thierry","last_name":"Bertomeu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mary","last_name":"McQuaid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Sánchez-Osuna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Papadopoli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daina","last_name":"Avizonis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariana","last_name":"De Sa Tavares Russo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Huard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Topisirovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hugo","last_name":"Wurtele","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mike","last_name":"Tyers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lea","last_name":"Harrington","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","High-Throughput Screening","High-Throughput Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vth6c4x9pp","title":"A single RNA polymerase II ubiquitylation site coordinates transcription with the DNA damage response","description":"In response to transcription-blocking DNA damage, cells orchestrate a multi-pronged\nreaction, involving transcription-coupled DNA repair, degradation of RNAPII and\ngenome-wide transcription shutdown. How these responses are connected has\nremained unclear. Here we show that damage-induced ubiquitylation of RNAPII itself,\nat a single lysine (RPB1 K1268), is the focal point for DNA damage response\ncoordination. K1268-ubiquitylation affects DNA repair and signals RNAPII degradation,\nessential for surviving genotoxic insult. It is also crucial for transcriptional shutdown,\nin the absence of which cells display dramatic transcriptome alterations. Additionally,\nregulation of RNAPII stability is central to transcription recovery – indeed, depletion of\nthe RNAPII pool underlies the failure of this process in Cockayne syndrome B cells.\nThese data expose regulation of global RNAPII levels as integral to the cellular DNA\ndamage response, and open the intriguing possibility that RNAPII pool size generally\naffects cell-specific transcription programmes, in genome instability disorders and\neven normal cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-20T16:44:11.686Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vth6c4x9pp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vth6c4x9pp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ana","last_name":"Tufegdzic Vidakovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vesela","last_name":"Encheva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bram","last_name":"Snijders","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jesper","last_name":"Svejstrup","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Expression Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3hxdrzfvsw","title":"Transcription-coupled AID Deamination Damage Depends on ELOF1-associated RNA Polymerase II","description":"In adaptive immunity, transcription-coupled damage is introduced into antibody genes by activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID) to diversify antibody repertoire. However, the coordination between transcription and DNA damage/repair remains elusive. Here, we find that transcription elongation factor 1 (ELOF1) stabilizes paused RNA polymerase II (RNAPII) at transcription barriers, providing a platform for transcription-coupled DNA damage/repair. Using a genetic screen, we discover that ELOF1 is required for AID targeting and that ELOF1 deficiency results in defective antibody class switch recombination and somatic hypermutation in mice. While downstream transcription-coupled repair factors are dispensable for AID-damage, ELOF1 mechanistically facilitates both transcription-coupled damage and repair by stabilizing chromatin-bound RNAPII. In ELOF1-deficient cells, paused RNAPII tends to detach from chromatin and fails to recruit factors to induce or repair DNA damage. Our study places ELOF1 at the center of transcription-coupled DNA metabolism processes and suggests a transition of RNAPII from elongation to a DNA damage/repair scaffold.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-05T15:02:00.618Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3hxdrzfvsw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3hxdrzfvsw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"鹏飞","last_name":"代","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yifeng","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ztwnydbhsw","title":"G2 arrest primes hematopoietic stem cells for megakaryopoiesis","description":"In contrast to most hematopoietic lineages, megakaryocytes (MKs) can derive rapidly and directly from hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs). The underlying mechanism is unclear however. Here we show that DNA damage induces MK markers in HSCs and that G2 arrest, an integral part of the DNA damage response, suffices for MK priming followed by irreversible MK differentiation in HSCs, but not in progenitors. We also show that replication stress causes DNA damage in HSCs and is at least in part due to uracil misincorporation in vitro and in vivo.  Consistent with this notion, thymidine attenuated DNA damage, improved HSC maintenance and reduced the generation of CD41+ MK-committed HSCs. Replication stress and concomitant MK differentiation is therefore one of the barriers to HSC maintenance. DNA damage-induced MK priming may allow rapid generation of a lineage essential to immediate organismal survival, while also removing damaged cells from the HSC pool. \nThis dataset accompanies a manuscript in Cell Report, 2024, and contain the data from ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-05T06:57:20.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztwnydbhsw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztwnydbhsw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hans","last_name":"Snoeck","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Cycle","DNA Damage Response","Hematopoietic Stem Cell","General Hematopoiesis","Megakaryocytopoiesis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wpgk3sbskj","title":"Sch9S6K controls DNA repair and DNA damage response efficiency in aging cells. Lucca et al.","description":"Abstract \nSurvival from UV-induced DNA lesions relies on nucleotide excision repair (NER) and the Mec1ATR DNA damage response (DDR). We study DDR and NER in aging cells and find that old cells struggle to repair DNA and\nactivate Mec1ATR. We employ pharmacological and genetic approaches to rescue DDR and NER during aging. Conditions activating Snf1AMPK rescue DDR functionality, but not NER, while inhibition of the TORC1-\nSch9S6K axis restores NER and enhances DDR by tuning PP2A activity, specifically in aging cells. Age-related\nrepair deficiency depends on Snf1AMPK-mediated phosphorylation of Sch9S6K on Ser160 and Ser163. PP2A\nactivity in old cells is detrimental for DDR and influences NER by modulating Snf1AMPK and Sch9S6K. Hence,\nthe DDR and repair pathways in aging cells are influenced by the metabolic tuning of opposing AMPK and\nTORC1 networks and by PP2A activity. Specific Sch9S6K phospho-isoforms control DDR and NER efficiency,\nspecifically during aging.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-31T08:01:33.723Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wpgk3sbskj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wpgk3sbskj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elisa","last_name":"Ferrari","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Repair","Protein Phosphatase","Aging","Saccharomyces cerevisiae","Ribosomal Protein S6 Kinase Beta-1"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kf7jknsvn4","title":"SNIP1 recruits TET2 to regulate c-MYC target genes and cellular DNA damage response","description":"The TET2 DNA dioxygenase regulates gene expression by catalyzing demethylation of 5-methylcytosine, thus epigenetically modulating the genome. TET2 does not contain a sequence-specific DNA binding domain and how it is recruited to specific genomic sites is not fully understood. Here we carried out a mammalian two-hybrid screen and identified multiple transcriptional regulators that potentially interact with TET2. The SMAD nuclear interacting protein 1 (SNIP1) physically interacts with TET2 and bridges TET2 to bind several transcription factors including c-MYC. SNIP1 recruits TET2 to the promoters of c-MYC target genes, including those involved in DNA damage response and cell viability. TET2 protects cells from DNA damage-induced apoptosis in a manner dependent on SNIP1. Our observations uncover a mechanism for targeting TET2 to specific promoters through a ternary interaction with a co-activator and many sequence-specific DNA binding factors. This study also reveals a TET2-SNIP1-c-MYC pathway in mediating DNA damage response, thereby connecting epigenetic control to maintenance of genome stability.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-09-27T07:30:18.605Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kf7jknsvn4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kf7jknsvn4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"LEILEI","last_name":"CHEN","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gel"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jfhwwhczx8","title":"PP2A controls genome integrity by integrating nutrient sensing and metabolic pathways with the DNA damage response_Ferrari et al.2017","description":"Synthetic genetic array (SGA), Metabolomics and Unprocessed data represented in the paper \"PP2A controls genome integrity by integrating nutrient sensing and metabolic pathways with the DNA damage response\" by Ferrari et al. 2017.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-05-31T08:38:59.015Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jfhwwhczx8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jfhwwhczx8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elisa","last_name":"Ferrari","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","Oligonucleotide","Western Blot","Metabolism","Image Analysis (Medical Imaging)","Sensitivity Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xvd7h85tm","title":"Heterochromatin-encoded Satellite RNAs Induce Breast Cancer","description":"Heterochromatic repetitive satellite RNAs are extensively transcribed in a variety of human cancers, including BRCA1-mutant breast cancer. Aberrant expression of satellite RNAs in cultured cells induces the DNA damage response, activates cell cycle checkpoints, and causes defects in chromosome segregation. However, the mechanism by which satellite RNA expression leads to genomic instability is not well understood. Here, we provide the first evidence that increased levels of satellite RNAs in mammary glands induce tumor formation in mice. Using mass spectrometry, we further show that genomic instability induced by satellite RNAs occurs through interactions with BRCA1-associated protein networks required for the stabilization of DNA replication forks. Additionally formation of RNA-DNA hybrids contributes to DNA replication defects in cells expressing satellite RNAs. These studies lay the foundation for developing novel therapeutic strategies that block the effects of non-coding satellite RNAs in cancer cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-16T03:38:25.161Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xvd7h85tm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xvd7h85tm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Quan","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"kngk6pp5m6","title":"Topical treatment with KS15 and SR8278 alters mRNA expression of XPA and WEE1 in human skin explants","description":"Analysis of gene expression of a small set of circadian and DNA damage response genes in 16 human skin explants treated with vehicle (DMSO) or a combination of the cryptochrome inhibitor KS15 and REV-ERB antagonist SR8278.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-08T12:44:57.351Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kngk6pp5m6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kngk6pp5m6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Kemp","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Skin","Circadian Rhythm","Gene Expression","DNA Damage Response","UV Radiation"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":143590,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":158,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":143590,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":142794,"restricted":71,"embargoed":725},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2358},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1443},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":702},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":483},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":455},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":424},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":394},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":341},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":282}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":140920},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1914},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":141},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":80},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":938},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":881},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":537},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":372}]}}